description: Journeys.API controller and HTTP contract rules
globs: Journeys.API/**
---

# API / controllers

- No business logic in controllers: validate route/body, call one service (or a small justified orchestration), map to HTTP.
- Prefer `ICampaignService`-style injection over injecting `I*Adapter` directly unless there is a documented exception. New code defaults to services only.
- Use `ActionResult<T>` / `IActionResult` with `ProducesResponseType` where OpenAPI clarity matters.
- Prefer `CancellationToken` on async actions when the service stack supports it.
- Validation errors: throw `APIErrorsException` with `Dictionary<string, string>` (aligned with `GlobalExceptionMiddleware`).
- Unexpected errors: rely on `GlobalExceptionMiddleware`; if a controller still catches, log with `ILogger` and return 500 with a safe message.
- Enums serialize as strings (`JsonStringEnumConverter` in `Program.cs`); keep new DTO enums consistent.
- Keep `Program.cs` composition thin: extension methods (`AddDAL`, `AddInfra`, `AddCampaignServices`), not dozens of inline registrations.
- MCP, A2A, and CampaignAgent handlers call the same services as REST — no duplicated business logic in tool handlers.
